L1053: **Figs. 3E-F. Effect of vitamin C on the 0-min postexercise FEF60(P)**
L1054: In Figs. 3E and 3F the dash lines indicate the equality between vitamin C and placebo.
L1055: If vitamin C has no effect, the observations would be located randomly and symmetrically
L1056: on both sides of the dash lines. The continuous lines indicate the regression lines.
L1057: The numbers identify the participants with the same numbers as used by S&S.
L1058: **3E: Vitamin C effect on postexercise FEF60(P) change.**
L1059: This figure shows the effect of vitamin C in percentage points (pp).
L1060: For example, on the placebo-day, participant #11 had a FEF60(P) change of -16.67%,
L1061: and on the vitamin C day a FEF60(P) change of +88.89%,
L1062: which gives the 105 pp improvement shown in Fig. 3E.
L1063: Slope of the linear regression model indicates that postexercise FEF60(P) change was decreased
L1064: by 71% (95% CI: -3% to 145%, P = 0.06) on the vitamin C day compared with the change on the
L1065: placebo day. Addition of the placebo-day postexercise FEF60(P) change to the model containing the
L1066: intercept improved the model fit by χ [2] (1 df) = 4.4 (P = 0.035).
L1067: **3F: Vitamin C effect on postexercise FEF60(P) level.**
L1068: This figure shows the effect of vitamin C in percentages.
L1069: For example, on the placebo-day, participant #11 had a postexercise FEF60(P) level of 2.5 L/s,
L1070: and on the vitamin C day a postexercise FEF60(P) level of 3.4 L/s,
L1071: which gives the 36% increase in the postexercise FEF60(P) level shown in Fig. 3F.
L1072: Slope of the linear regression model indicates that vitamin C had a significant effect on the
L1073: postexercise FEF60(P) level (P = 0.02). Addition of the placebo-day postexercise FEF60(P) level to the
L1074: model containing the intercept improved the model fit by χ [2] (1 df) = 6.6 (P = 0.01).
L1075: 7
